WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.080
What’s up, Wisecrack?

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.710
You’ve been asking for it, and it’s finally
time.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:11.200
This week we’re diving into the insanely
smart Neon Genesis Evangelion.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.000
Eva is a phenomenally complex story.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.770
On the surface it’s about the end of the
world and teenagers in enormous biomechanical

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:22.029
monsters… who fight giant alien-angel-robot…
things.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:27.009
Beneath that, there’s an investigation of
psychosexual development, intimacy, unconscious

00:00:27.009 --> 00:00:30.509
human motivation, and how hard it is to be
a person.

00:00:30.509 --> 00:00:33.560
But the real meat of everything is about…
hedgehogs.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.560
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy
of Neon Genesis Evangelion.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.560
And, if you haven’t seen it, spoilers ahead.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.800
Just a quick note: even though the Eva reboot
is beautiful, we decided to keep our analysis

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.180
to the original twenty-six-episode series
and the films (End of Evangelion, and Death

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.180
and Rebirth).

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.190
And, if you like this, maybe we’ll come
back for more.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.030
So first, a quick recap.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:57.780
In the distant future of 2015 the earth is
under attack by unearthly beings called Angels.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.229
In order to combat them, humanity has created
ass-kicking robots that sync with their pilots

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:03.530
called Evas.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.780
Of course, the Evas aren’t all they seem.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.830
They’re actually former Angels infused with
the souls of humans — “Inside the empty

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:15.010
shell of each Eva is a human soul," — specifically
the mothers of the various pilots.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.010
"Mother?"

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:21.330
Shinji, Asuka, and Rei (who is a clone) work
together to pilot the Evas, save the world,

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.230
and come to terms with their inner demons.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.360
Oh, also, Rei bonds the DNA of original angels
Adam and Lillith, becomes a being containing

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.390
the consciousness of all humanity, and portends
the end of existence, but don’t worry, we’ll

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.390
cover that.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.489
To understand the ending of Evangelion, we
have to dive into some complicated ideas,

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:39.740
but I promise, it’ll be fun.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:45.010
Part one: Eschatology and Mysticism
Neon Genesis Evangelion literally means a

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.380
new beginning.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.940
Neon is derived from the greek neos which
means “new,” Genesis is a beginning and

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.210
a reference to the opening book of the bible,
and Evangelion is the Gospel or the good news

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.050
- from the same root as evangelize.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:03.709
So Neon Genesis Evangelion is a new telling
of the gospel: genesis.

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:07.900
Or the easy way: it’s a re-imagining of
the book of Genesis with lots of technology,

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.780
brooding, and sex.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.629
Eva is overflowing with religious iconography.

00:03:12.629 --> 00:03:16.780
Some of the references are easy to spot: the
show references the dead sea scrolls explicitly

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.379
— "Incidents not predicted in the dead sea
scrolls may occur.

00:03:20.379 --> 00:03:25.170
This should teach the old men a lesson," — and
Ritzgo installs a 666-type firewall to protect

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.790
the Magi from a cyberattack.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:31.620
“Ikari has installed a type 666 firewall
on the Magi’s external feed circuits.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.379
That’s very difficult to breach.”

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:37.690
Not to mention the presence of creatures called
angels, two of which are named Adam and Lillith.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:41.849
You might have expected Eve, but Lilith is
Adam’s female counterpart.

00:03:41.849 --> 00:03:45.940
According to Jewish folklore, She was created
around the same time as Adam and is usually

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.069
described as being a mother of demons.

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:49.620
You know — evil.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:52.290
In Eva, humans are the descendants of Lilith.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.680
Some of the religious work is Christian in
nature.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:59.580
The magi system, the super computer network
that runs NERV, has three parts, each named

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.540
after the three wise men, Casper, Balthasar,
and Melchior.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:06.860
Ya know, the guys that visited baby Jesus
to give him the gifts of Goldeneye, Frankenstein,

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:07.909
and Helen Mirren.

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:12.659
But the majority of the religious imagery
is based on Jewish mysticism and the Kaballah.

00:04:12.659 --> 00:04:17.299
Kaballah is an investigative practice of faith
and mystical teachings that seeks religious

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:19.479
revelation and sacred knowledge.

00:04:19.479 --> 00:04:23.720
Some of its biggest fans are ancient rabbis,
and also Madonna.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.949
Most of the angels in Eva are taken directly
from texts in Judaism’s extended universe

00:04:27.949 --> 00:04:33.040
like the Zohar (essentially the Kaballa’s
bible), the Talmud and the book of Enoch.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.500
Others are written in el theosophy — a form
of mystical writing that uses the “el”

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.620
suffix, which is one of the many Hebrew words
for god, to tie names to the divine, and it’s

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:46.900
all over Evangelion: Ramiel, Sachiel, Matarael,
and Galadriel.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.630
Oh, sorry, scratch that one.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:52.690
In Kabbalistic tradition, the first being
is Adam Kadmon, not to be confused with the

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:57.380
other famous Adam, a being of light — which
is eerily similar to the giant of light that

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:02.060
is Eva’s first angel, named Adam, who explodes
in the second impact.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:07.960
That second impact kills half the population
of the planet and turns the oceans red revelation-style.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.901
This destruction is another call back to the
Kabbalistic tradition, an event called the

00:05:11.901 --> 00:05:14.580
Shevirat ha-Kelim or the breaking of vessels.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:20.310
See, in the beginning everything was connected,
part of a universal unity that broke apart

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:24.380
leaving everything separate and creating the
many-splendored world we live in.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.110
It’s sort of like a theological big bang.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:32.300
The purpose of Kaballah is Tikkun ha-Olam
or the restoration of the world—it’s traditionally

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:37.770
practiced through acts of good deeds and prayer
— but in Eva it is sort of taken literally.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:43.050
The ethical call of the kaballa is the reunification
of the world, of the different fragmented

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.759
parts of Adam, called the Sefirot.

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:50.490
Which is depicted as the tree of life—as
ten circles in a particular pattern.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:54.259
It creates a sort of visual tree that is meant
to depict the tree of life.

00:05:54.259 --> 00:05:58.759
It’s why the random circles appear around
Eva Unit one when it is attacked by the nine

00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:03.930
manufactured angels — they’re all fulfilling
the sacred duty or purpose of the kaballa.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:09.710
The Sefirot reunifies everything, recreating
the universe as a complete whole, and in doing

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:15.289
so, destroys the individuality and separation
that defines life as we know it.

00:06:15.289 --> 00:06:20.130
Many characters pursue the goal of literally
reunifying the world, putting all the different

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:22.289
consciousnesses into one place.

00:06:22.289 --> 00:06:29.509
"Through indiscriminate death and through
prayer, we will return to our original state,

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:32.130
and our souls will be at peace."

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:36.680
This finally happens after the Adam and Lillith
DNA combine inside of Rei.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:41.710
The reunification with the masculine and feminine
aspects of the angels is the reunification

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.460
of the world as well.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:47.800
This raises the question of why exactly the
world needs to be reunited.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.270
What does it mean for the world to be broken?

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:54.850
To answer that, we need only turn to our adorable
friend the hedgehog.

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:56.600
And no, not that one.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:01.580
Part two: The Hedgehog’s Dilemma
So much of Evangelion is eye candy.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:02.580
Sex.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:03.580
Big Robots.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:04.580
Explosions.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:05.580
More sex.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:06.580
The apocalypse.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:11.800
But it’s at its most profound when the narrative
dives into crimson red lakes of sadness, isolation,

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:12.910
and the human condition.

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:18.440
"I'm seeing these for the first time, and
yet this is not the first time."

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:23.261
It pauses on empty streets, on telephone poles
where nothing scares the birds away, and on

00:07:23.261 --> 00:07:25.420
classrooms with no students.

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:28.800
The world without half its population is a
sad place.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.110
The end of times isn’t partying like it’s
1999 — it just sucks.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:37.820
The characters’ miserable lives and intimacy
problems can be explained by a hedgehog and

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:41.419
the preeminent philosophical pessimist Arthur
Schopenhauer.

00:07:41.419 --> 00:07:44.940
In his book Parega and Paralipomena: Short
Philosophical Essays.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:50.080
Volume 2, Schopenhauer details a short parable
that has become known as the hedgehog or the

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:51.870
porcupine’s dilemma.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:56.389
In it, he explains the human condition by
comparing humans to hedgehogs huddled together

00:07:56.389 --> 00:07:57.520
for warmth.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.990
Though they crave warmth, hedgehogs can’t
get too close because of their quills.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:07.069
So, they keep their distance and remain cold
or they learn to live without the warmth of

00:08:07.069 --> 00:08:08.069
others.

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:09.900
What does this have to do with Eva?

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.139
Well, everything.

00:08:11.139 --> 00:08:14.069
The hedgehog’s dilemma is Eva’s guiding
theme.

00:08:14.069 --> 00:08:18.639
In episode three, Ritsuko explains the parable
to Mitsado, "Do you know the fable ‘The

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:19.639
Hedgehog's Dilemma?’”

00:08:19.639 --> 00:08:20.639
"Hedgehog?

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:23.240
You mean those animals with the spiny hair?"

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:28.100
"Even though a headgehog may wanna become
close with another hedgehog, the closer they

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:30.569
get the more they injure each other with their
spines.

00:08:30.569 --> 00:08:36.129
It's the same with some humans," and Episode
four is even called “The Hedgehog’s Dilemma.”

00:08:36.129 --> 00:08:39.899
That desire to be unhurt drives a wedge between
people.

00:08:39.899 --> 00:08:42.159
It creates a world of despair.

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:47.470
We see that world, and its implications in
Episode sixteen titled, “sickness unto death”

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:50.160
which is a reference to Danish philosopher
Soren Kierkegaard.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:56.260
A sickness unto death is despair — a terrible
existential loneliness, which goes along with

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:00.970
Schopenhauer’s hedgehogs pretty neatly;
as does Episode twenty three.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:05.089
After Kaji is killed and he leaves a sad message
on Mitsado’s machine, she remarks: “Another

00:09:05.089 --> 00:09:07.429
phone that will never ring.”

00:09:07.429 --> 00:09:11.970
It’s the epitome of lonesomeness — waiting
for someone to reach out and call.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:15.870
It’s a depressing show, filled with depressed
people.

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.450
Asuka pilots her Eva to find meaning in life.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:22.440
Without it, she’s just a sad girl crying
in a dingy apartment.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.850
We see in flashbacks thatm though she may
seem gruff, she fears being alone, and pilots

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:31.589
her Eva to draw people toward her and expand
her world.

00:09:31.589 --> 00:09:33.970
“Everybody’s so nice to me now!

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:35.959
I don’t feel lonely anymore!”

00:09:35.959 --> 00:09:40.269
Then there’s Gendo, who struggles with his
ability to show Shinji love and doesn’t

00:09:40.269 --> 00:09:41.649
know how to be a father.

00:09:41.649 --> 00:09:45.760
"When Shinji is near me, all I ever do is
cause him pain."

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:50.410
When he’s reunited with Yui, he explains
that — “I never deserved to be loved.”

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:53.649
— so he rejected the world so he wouldn’t
feel pain.

00:09:53.649 --> 00:09:58.769
In his book “The World as Will and Representation,”
Schopenhauer argues that life is suffering

00:09:58.769 --> 00:10:02.519
because the world is made up of will and representation.

00:10:02.519 --> 00:10:05.610
For Schopenhauer, the nature of reality is
unity.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:10.499
We’d all be at peace without the struggle
to communicate between individuals.

00:10:10.499 --> 00:10:12.690
Suffering is the result of individuality.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:18.040
To be human is to suffer because communication,
perception, and understanding will never be

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:22.369
perfect and that struggle to connect is the
source of our suffering.

00:10:22.369 --> 00:10:28.209
The universe is really one thing in itself
- one entity — but each individual will

00:10:28.209 --> 00:10:32.579
or ego can only perceive the world from its
own vantage point or perception.

00:10:32.579 --> 00:10:36.300
That’s what Schopenhauer means when he says
representation.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:42.529
Anguish, fragmentation, alienation, and isolation
are all because each person is in charge of

00:10:42.529 --> 00:10:43.630
their will.

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:47.950
And there is a disconnect between what we
desire and what the world gives us.

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:52.089
Which is echoed by Kaworu: "This is mankind's
fate.

00:10:52.089 --> 00:10:54.949
The thread of human hope is spun with the
flax of sorrow."

00:10:54.949 --> 00:10:59.569
That separation of wills is what the show
calls AT-fields, which are something like

00:10:59.569 --> 00:11:03.040
the unique human soul that separates each
person’s ego.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.279
It’s sort of their life force.

00:11:05.279 --> 00:11:08.720
The barrier that keeps us from truly connecting
to each other.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:13.110
Several times characters have conversations
about what it means to be an ego and to have

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:17.029
a body — usually as their body or consciousness
is slipping away.

00:11:17.029 --> 00:11:20.410
Rei does this in episodes 14 and 23.

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:25.549
Both exchanges are reflections on what it
means to be a human being that has a body

00:11:25.549 --> 00:11:27.600
and is recognized by others.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:28.620
“You're right.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:30.449
At the core we are all the same.”

00:11:30.449 --> 00:11:31.839
“Our minds lack something basic.”

00:11:31.839 --> 00:11:33.399
”And we fear that lack.”

00:11:33.399 --> 00:11:34.399
“We fear it.”

00:11:34.399 --> 00:11:37.060
“And that is why we are attempting to become
one.”

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:39.600
“We will melt with and fill each other.”

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:41.959
“This is the instrumentality.”

00:11:41.959 --> 00:11:44.920
“Mankind cannot live without being surrounded
by others.”

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:46.799
“Mankind cannot live alone.”

00:11:46.799 --> 00:11:48.899
“Although you yourself are always unique.”

00:11:48.899 --> 00:11:50.209
“That is why life is hard.”

00:11:50.209 --> 00:11:51.369
“That is why life is sad and empty.”

00:11:51.369 --> 00:11:55.959
“Thus, you want the close physical and mental
presence of others.”

00:11:55.959 --> 00:11:57.540
“That is why we wish to become one.”

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:02.459
This struggle with isolation and the pain
of rejection defines Eva.

00:12:02.459 --> 00:12:07.749
Unfortunately, as Schopenhauer tells us, being
alive means living with will and ego, and

00:12:07.749 --> 00:12:09.350
that means pain.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:13.869
That drive to get away from this pain and
ego is something we can explain with the help

00:12:13.869 --> 00:12:17.089
of our cocaine-addled friend Dr. Freud.

00:12:17.089 --> 00:12:21.079
Part three: Psychoanalysis
I know people are mad because Freud is considered

00:12:21.079 --> 00:12:22.939
a hack and didn’t science hard enough.

00:12:22.939 --> 00:12:23.939
I get it.

00:12:23.939 --> 00:12:29.449
But, hey - Eva explicitly uses Freudian terms,
ideas, and analysis.

00:12:29.449 --> 00:12:33.980
Episode nineteen is called introjection, a
Freudian term that explains how people take

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:36.560
on the characteristics of the people around
them.

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:38.199
You know copy cats.

00:12:38.199 --> 00:12:41.549
Episode twenty is called Weaving a Story 2:
Oral Stage.

00:12:41.549 --> 00:12:45.470
The Oral Stage corresponds to the weaning
of a child from breastfeeding.

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:49.319
According to Freud, wean a child too soon
and they’re out of touch with people’s

00:12:49.319 --> 00:12:51.749
emotions and are generally sociopathic.

00:12:51.749 --> 00:12:55.490
Ween them too late and they’re dependent
too long on the mother for security and end

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:57.589
up neurotic mama’s boys.

00:12:57.589 --> 00:12:59.989
Like that Robin Arryn kid from Game of Thrones.

00:12:59.989 --> 00:13:03.989
All the kids in the Eva’s pilot classes
are motherless children, or so they think.

00:13:03.989 --> 00:13:08.339
In fact, Shinji’s mom is Unit one’s soul
and part of Asuka’s mother’s soul is in

00:13:08.339 --> 00:13:09.339
Unit two.

00:13:09.339 --> 00:13:13.439
Both Evas use the souls inside them to further
bond with their pilots.

00:13:13.439 --> 00:13:17.189
How the pilot reacts to their mother is indicative
of their general disposition.

00:13:17.189 --> 00:13:22.199
It’s as Freudian as it can get — they’re
all mourning the lost love object that is

00:13:22.199 --> 00:13:23.470
their mother.

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:28.600
Except Rei is less mournful and more melancholic
because she has no soul.

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:33.720
As an artificial being, she has no real loss
to speak of; no mama soul to snatch and put

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:34.749
in a robot.

00:13:34.749 --> 00:13:40.110
For Freud, mourning happens when we know what
we have lost — melancholia is much more

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:44.910
serious — it occurs when people have no
idea what’s missing, only that it causes

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:48.279
them deep, deep sadness (hence the need for
psychotherapy.)

00:13:48.279 --> 00:13:52.540
That’s why the last two episodes of the
show are literally a psychotherapy session

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:53.899
inside Shinji’s mind.

00:13:53.899 --> 00:13:58.160
“But if you depend only on the Eva, then
the Eva will become your only identifier.”

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:00.699
“Eva will become your only value.”

00:14:00.699 --> 00:14:04.980
“Without that value you —&nbsp;the real you
— will be erased from this existence.”

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:08.660
We mentioned the Magi supercomputers before,
but we didn’t tell you that they have the

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:11.670
mind of Dr. Naoko Akagi imprinted in them.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:15.639
They are reflective of the tri-part model
of the human unconscious: the controlling

00:14:15.639 --> 00:14:21.360
scientist as the ego, the mother as the punishing
and guilt inducing superego, and the woman

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.319
as the sexual id.

00:14:23.319 --> 00:14:28.739
Episode eighteen is titled ambivalence - a
Freudian idea that Eva really leans into.

00:14:28.739 --> 00:14:33.179
Ambivalence is the way that people can passionately
hate and love at the same time.

00:14:33.179 --> 00:14:35.009
Like when I eat Chipotle.

00:14:35.009 --> 00:14:39.410
For example, Shinji is constantly talking
about how much he hates his father — "However,

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:44.999
I really hated my father," — yet he gets
in the Eva over and over to make Daddy proud.

00:14:44.999 --> 00:14:49.309
In episode twelve he explains that he pilots
the Eva to hear words of praise from his dad.

00:14:49.309 --> 00:14:53.959
"I think I finally understood the good part
about being praised by someone when I heard

00:14:53.959 --> 00:14:56.129
my father's words, today.

00:14:56.129 --> 00:15:02.769
And, I also realized that the reason I pilot
my eva is to hear those words from him."

00:15:02.769 --> 00:15:07.069
He runs away from his dad, NERV, and his responsibilities
as an Eva pilot.

00:15:07.069 --> 00:15:08.720
But he keeps coming back.

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.399
"I mustn't run away.

00:15:10.399 --> 00:15:12.239
I mustn't not run away."

00:15:12.239 --> 00:15:17.339
But the real smart stuff is done with Eros
and Thanatos — or the drive to life and

00:15:17.339 --> 00:15:18.459
the death drive.

00:15:18.459 --> 00:15:23.160
For Freud, beings are instilled with the pleasure
principle — the general inclination to avoid

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:25.040
pain and maximize pleasure.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:30.449
So, in episode sixteen, when Shinji falls
into a Dirac sea (or a pocket universe) he

00:15:30.449 --> 00:15:34.279
laments, "This world is filled with too much
pain and suffering to keep going on," he is

00:15:34.279 --> 00:15:39.120
expressing his desire to minimize suffering
as it pertains to the pleasure principle.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:43.939
This struggle to avoid pain is aligned with
Schopenhauer’s ideas about will and struggle.

00:15:43.939 --> 00:15:48.449
How can we ever avoid suffering when we have
to share a world with other people, all of

00:15:48.449 --> 00:15:51.939
whom have their own desires that sometimes
get in the way of ours?

00:15:51.939 --> 00:15:53.790
How do we find peace?

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:59.870
For Seele, NERV, and even individuals like
Gendo, and Shinji it all comes back to instrumentalization,

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:04.739
the process by which all human consciousness
is combined into a primordial soup called

00:16:04.739 --> 00:16:05.829
LCL.

00:16:05.829 --> 00:16:10.489
That desire to join a unified whole and no
longer be concerned with issues of self comes

00:16:10.489 --> 00:16:11.679
up in Freud, too.

00:16:11.679 --> 00:16:13.689
It’s called The Death Drive.

00:16:13.689 --> 00:16:18.790
The death drive is a desire to return to tranquility
— to the peace and harmony of the womb.

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:21.660
And what can be more peaceful than the end
of everything?

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:25.069
Once it’s gone there’s nothing to worry
about.

00:16:25.069 --> 00:16:30.049
Episode 25 has a direct call to this with
the text: “the case of Shinji Ikari, the

00:16:30.049 --> 00:16:32.579
boy who wished for death of his own will".

00:16:32.579 --> 00:16:37.139
Then Gendo, in the mind of Shinji, states
that instrumentality: “is not a return to

00:16:37.139 --> 00:16:38.170
nothingness.

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:40.930
It is merely a return to the state of beginning.

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:45.489
It is no less than a return to the primal
womb that we lost so long ago.

00:16:45.489 --> 00:16:48.600
Souls and minds will become one, attaining
eternal balance.”

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:51.329
A return to Lilith and Tikkun ha-Olam.

00:16:51.329 --> 00:16:55.649
The restoration of the world— or the reunification
with the primordial being.

00:16:55.649 --> 00:17:00.130
And so Seele and Nerv are working for the
death drive and they count on Shinji to become

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:05.309
the arc for humanity that which will allow
them to return to a world without loneliness

00:17:05.309 --> 00:17:06.610
and isolation.

00:17:06.610 --> 00:17:09.569
Conclusion
So the human reaction to the hedgehog’s

00:17:09.569 --> 00:17:14.120
dilemma is to seek tranquility in a return
to the womb, but as we’ve seen, that’s

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:18.620
simply a desire for death, a running away,
so what is to be done?

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:24.329
Well, it’s Kaworu, a character who is not
entirely human, that shows us the way.

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:25.919
Accepting vulnerability.

00:17:25.919 --> 00:17:31.299
Kaworu gives up his individual desires and
will so that Shinji can live; he is unguarded

00:17:31.299 --> 00:17:34.279
and open with his absolute love for Shinji.

00:17:34.279 --> 00:17:38.580
He represents a way out of the cycle of constant
suffering and isolation.

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:44.190
And he just gives damned good advice: "I know
that by keeping others at a distance you avoid

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.299
a betrayal of your trust.

00:17:46.299 --> 00:17:49.330
For while you may not be hurt that way, you
mustn’t forget that you must endure the

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:50.330
loneliness.

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:57.970
Man cannot completely erase this sadness because
all men are fundamentally alone.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:04.590
Pain is something that man must endure in
his heart, and since the heart feels pain

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:07.059
so easily some believe life is pain.”

00:18:07.059 --> 00:18:12.490
In the final episodes, and the movie, Shinji
initially accepts instrumentalization, but

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:17.590
when he understands just what he’d lose
… himself, he has a change of heart.

00:18:17.590 --> 00:18:21.230
“Your self-image is restrained by having
to observe the barriers between yourself,

00:18:21.230 --> 00:18:22.230
and others.”

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:25.970
“And yet, you cannot see yourself without
the presence of others.”

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:28.649
“Because there are others, I can perceive
myself as an individual!

00:18:28.649 --> 00:18:31.500
If I am alone, then I will be the same without
others!

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:34.880
For if this world is only of me, then there
will be no difference between me and nothing!”

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:37.429
At the end of the series Shinji rejects instrumentality.

00:18:37.429 --> 00:18:38.429
"I am me.

00:18:38.429 --> 00:18:41.840
I want to be myself.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:44.770
I want to continue existing in this world."

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:49.059
He renounces a world where everyone is primordial
soup and shares one mind.

00:18:49.059 --> 00:18:50.640
"What is this...

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:52.230
An empty space?

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:53.809
An empty world?

00:18:53.809 --> 00:18:56.450
A world where nothing exists but myself?

00:18:56.450 --> 00:19:00.400
But with only myself, I have nothing to interact
with!

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:03.360
It's as if I'm here, but not here at all!

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:06.040
It's as if I'm slowly fading out of existence..."

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:12.280
He opts for one where people can be hurt,
where we aren’t close, where there is separation

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:17.570
and individual selves, where there are individual
wills, desires, and pain.

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:22.649
He confronts the drive to inorganic existence
and accepts the inevitability of suffering

00:19:22.649 --> 00:19:23.649
in life.

00:19:23.649 --> 00:19:24.649
“That's correct.

00:19:24.649 --> 00:19:26.020
If the pain is too harsh, you can escape it.”

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:28.720
“If you really hate it, Shinji, you can
still run.”

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:29.720
“No!

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:30.950
I won't!

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:33.929
I'm tired of escaping!

00:19:33.929 --> 00:19:36.130
Yes -- I mustn't run away!”

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:38.590
But he also opens the door for hope and joy.

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:39.590
He opts for a world where we he can hurt and
be hurt.

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:43.700
So the first thing he does when he rejects
instrumentality — he turns over and chokes

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:44.700
Asuka.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:49.799
She responds by stroking his face, reminding
us that the world they chose is one of violence

00:19:49.799 --> 00:19:52.580
and pain, but also love.

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:57.730
All three elements of the story: the theological,
psychological, and philosophical deal with

00:19:57.730 --> 00:19:59.690
something called lack.

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:05.480
In psychology lack, as explored by the Freud-inspired
psychoanalyst and philosopher Jacques Lacan,

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:10.659
is the missing center at the heart of our
existence, the missing piece between representation

00:20:10.659 --> 00:20:12.380
and the world in itself.

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:17.460
Whether it be a scarcity of friends, failing
marriages and relationships, or distance between

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:18.559
father and son.

00:20:18.559 --> 00:20:24.470
Eva is able to consistently weave a plot that
delves deep into the visceral reality of humanity’s

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:26.260
disappointing existence.

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:32.580
Three pilots: Rei, the Japanese word for soul,
ghost, not or zero, in unit zero.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:37.500
Shinji: the pilot of unit number one, the
first real person introduced, the other…

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:39.960
the pilot of unit number two… a girl.

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:44.960
A call back to the expulsion from Eden, the
world of infinite knowledge and life.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:47.299
In the end it’s just Asuka and Shinji.

00:20:47.299 --> 00:20:48.880
A new Adam and Eve.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:50.020
A new Beginning.

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:51.420
"How disgusting."

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:52.720
Hope you guys enjoyed this episode!

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:54.720
And as always, peace!

